<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hexham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hexham is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>. Around 674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> founded a monastery there whose church, built with stone from Roman sites, was among the finest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> England; its crypt survives under the present Hexham Abbey. It was briefly a bishopric, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-heavenfield">Battle of Heavenfield</a> of 634 was fought nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hexham.jpg" alt="Hexham" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hexham_-_Abbey_church_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
